As we know, threshold wavelength



\begin{array}{c}

\left(\lambda_{0}\right)=\frac{\mathrm{hc}}{\phi} \\

\Rightarrow \lambda_{0}=\frac{\left(6.63 \times 10^{-34}\right) \times 3 \times 10^{8}}{2.3 \times\left(1.6 \times 10^{-19}\right)}=5.404 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{~m}

\end{array}



$\Rightarrow \lambda_{0}=5404 Ã…$

Hence, wavelength $4144 Ã…$ and $4972 Ã…$ will emit electron from the metal surface. For each wavelength energy incident on the surface per unit time = intensity of each × area of the surface wavelength



=\frac{3.6 \times 10^{-3}}{3} \times(\mathrm{lcm})^{2}=1.2 \times 10^{-7} \text {joule }



Therefore, energy incident on the surface for each wavelength in $2 \mathrm{~s}$

\mathrm{E}=\left(1.2 \times 10^{-7}\right) \times 2=2.4 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{~J}



Number of photons $\mathrm{n}_{1}$ due to wavelength $4144 Ã…$

n_{1}=\frac{\left(2.4 \times 10^{-7}\right)\left(4144 \times 10^{-10}\right)}{\left(6.63 \times 10^{-34}\right)\left(3 \times 10^{8}\right)}=0.5 \times 10^{12}



Number of photons $\mathrm{n}_{2}$ due to the wavelength $4972 Ã…$

\mathrm{n}_{2}=\frac{\left(2.4 \times 10^{-7}\right)\left(4972 \times 10^{-10}\right)}{\left(6.63 \times 10^{-34}\right)\left(3 \times 10^{8}\right)}=0.572 \times 10^{12}



Therefore total number of photoelectrons liberated in $2 \mathrm{~s}$

\begin{aligned}

\mathrm{N} &=\mathrm{n}_{1}+\mathrm{n}_{2} \\

&=0.5 \times 10^{12}+0.575 \times 10^{12} \\

&=1.075 \times 10^{12}

\end{aligned}

As we know,



\mathrm{F}=\mathrm{qvB}=\mathrm{m} \frac{\mathrm{v}^{2}}{\mathrm{R}} \Rightarrow \mathrm{v}=\frac{\mathrm{q}}{\mathrm{m}} \mathrm{BR}



The kinetic energy of the photoelectron



\begin{array}{l}

=\frac{1}{2} m v^{2}=\frac{1}{2} \frac{e^{2} B^{2} R^{2}}{m} \\

=\frac{1}{2} \frac{\left(1.6 \times 10^{-19}\right)^{2}\left(2 \times 10^{-2}\right)^{2}\left(23 \times 10^{-3}\right)^{2}}{\left(9.1 \times 10^{-31}\right)} \\

=2.97 \times 10^{-15} \mathrm{~J} \\

=\frac{2.97 \times 10^{-15}}{1.6 \times 10^{-19}}=18.36 \mathrm{keV}

\end{array}



